Q: Installing the TailwindCSS Vite plugin with Vite 8 - peer vite@&quot;^5.2.0 || ^6 || ^7&quot; from @tailwindcss/vite@4.1 A: <p>I don't recommend using it in production projects until Vite 8 is officially released as a stable version. It's still in beta and under active development.</p>
<h3>Vite 8 with <code>@tailwindcss/vite</code></h3>
<p>I haven't verified it, but if the Vite API changes, the behavior of <code>@tailwindcss/vite</code> may change, become unpredictable, or break. For that reason, I'd treat it with some caution. In practice, however, it's the same installation step mentioned in the documentation, with one small override using a forced install to push dependencies that aren't compatible with Vite 8 alongside Vite 8.</p>
<p><strong>package.json</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;dependencies&quot;: {
    &quot;vite&quot;: &quot;8.0.0-beta.11&quot;,
    &quot;tailwindcss&quot;: &quot;^4&quot;,
    &quot;@tailwindcss/vite&quot;: &quot;^4&quot;
  },
  &quot;overrides&quot;: {
    &quot;@tailwindcss/vite&quot;: {
      &quot;vite&quot;: &quot;8.0.0-beta.11&quot;
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>vite.config.ts</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { defineConfig } from 'vite'
import tailwindcss from '@tailwindcss/vite'

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [
    tailwindcss(),
  ],
})
</code></pre>
<p>References:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss/discussions/19624" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>tailwindlabs/tailwindcss</code> discussion #19624</a></li>
<li><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/79677732/15167500">Installing the TailwindCSS Vite plugin with Vite 7 - peer vite@&quot;^5.2.0 || ^6&quot; from @tailwindcss/vite@4.1</a></li>
</ul>
<h3>Vite 8 with <code>@tailwindcss/postcss</code></h3>
<p>The Vite plugin is much more efficient, but in case of API breakages, <code>@tailwindcss/postcss</code> can be used as a fallback.</p>
<p><strong>package.json</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;dependencies&quot;: {
    &quot;vite&quot;: &quot;8.0.0-beta.11&quot;,
    &quot;postcss&quot;: &quot;^8&quot;,
    &quot;tailwindcss&quot;: &quot;^4&quot;,
    &quot;@tailwindcss/postcss&quot;: &quot;^4&quot;
  },
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>vite.config.ts</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { defineConfig } from 'vite'
import tailwindcss from '@tailwindcss/postcss'

export default defineConfig({
  css: {
    postcss: {
      plugins: [
        tailwindcss(),
      ],
    },
  },
})
</code></pre>
<h3>Known breaking change: using Lightning CSS instead of ESLint</h3>
<p>An important breaking change is that TailwindCSS uses LightningCSS under the hood, while Vite 8 also uses LightningCSS but with a different configuration (LightningCSS is configurable). TailwindCSS will have to address this issue and make its LightningCSS settings exportable so they can be applied in Vite. Until then, it's recommended to stick with the stable release.</p>
<p><strong>vite.config.ts</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { defineConfig } from &quot;vite&quot;;
import tailwindcss from &quot;@tailwindcss/vite&quot;;

export default defineConfig({
  build: {
    cssMinify: 'esbuild', // default: 'lightningcss'
  },
  plugins: [
    tailwindcss(),
  ],
});
</code></pre>
<p>References:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://vite.dev/guide/features#lightning-css" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://vite.dev/guide/features#lightning-css</a></li>
<li><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/79829663/15167500">OKLCH colors are converted to LAB colors when I start using Rolldown-Vite instead of Vite</a></li>
</ul>
